::GATE 2023 Q48::[plain]Let U \= \{1, 2, . . . , n\}, where n is a large positive integer greater than 1000. Let k be a positive integer less than n. Let A, B be subsets of U with |A| \= |B| \= k and A ∩ B \= ∅. We say that a permutation of U separates A from B if one of the following is true. - All members of A appear in the permutation before any of the members of B. - All members of B appear in the permutation before any of the members of A. How many permutations of U separate A from B? {~n! n 2k ~n 2k =(n − 2k)! n 2 2k (n − 2k)! (k!)2 (n − 2k)! (k!)2}
####Source: GATE 2023, Q48

::GATE 2023 Q49::[plain]Let f \: A → B be an onto (or surjective) function, where A and B are nonempty sets. Deﬁne an equivalence relation ∼ on the set A as a1 ∼ a2 if f (a1 ) \= f (a2 ) , where a1 , a2 ∈ A. Let E \= \{[x] \: x ∈ A\} be the set of all the equivalence classes under ∼. Deﬁne a new mapping F \: E → B as F ([x]) \= f (x), for all the equivalence classes [x] in E. Which of the following statements is/are TRUE? {~%-100%F is NOT well-deﬁned. ~%33.33%F is an onto (or surjective) function. ~%33.33%F is a one-to-one (or injective) function. ~%33.33%F is a bijective function.}
####Source: GATE 2023, Q49

::GATE 2023 Q51::[plain]Let X be a set and 2X denote the powerset of X. Deﬁne a binary operation Δ on 2X as follows\: AΔB \= (A − B) ∪ (B − A) . Let H \= (2X , Δ). Which of the following statements about H is/are correct? {~%50.0%H is a group. ~%-100%Every element in H has an inverse, but H is NOT a group. ~%-100%For every A ∈ 2X , the inverse of A is the complement of A. ~%50.0%For every A ∈ 2X , the inverse of A is A.}
####Source: GATE 2023, Q51

::GATE 2023 Q53::[plain]Consider a random experiment where two fair coins are tossed. Let A be the event that denotes HEAD on both the throws, B be the event that denotes HEAD on the ﬁrst throw, and C be the event that denotes HEAD on the second throw. Which of the following statements is/are TRUE? {~%-100%A and B are independent. ~%-100%A and C are independent. ~%50.0%B and C are independent. ~%50.0%Prob(B|C) \= Prob(B)}
####Source: GATE 2023, Q53

::GATE 2023 Q55::[plain]Let G be a simple, ﬁnite, undirected graph with vertex set \{v1 , . . . , vn \}. Let Δ(G) denote the maximum degree of G and let N \= \{1, 2, . . .\} denote the set of all possible colors. Color the vertices of G using the following greedy strategy\: for i \= 1, . . . , n color(vi ) ← min\{j ∈ N \: no neighbour of vi is colored j\} Which of the following statements is/are TRUE? {~%50.0%This procedure results in a proper vertex coloring of G. ~%50.0%The number of colors used is at most Δ(G) + 1. ~%-100%The number of colors used is at most Δ(G). ~%-100%The number of colors used is equal to the chromatic number of G.}
####Source: GATE 2023, Q55

::GATE 2024 Set 1 Q11::[plain]Let 𝑓\: ℝ → ℝ be a function such that 𝑓(𝑥) \= max\{𝑥, 𝑥 3 \}, 𝑥 ∈ ℝ , where ℝ is the set of all real numbers. The set of all points where 𝑓(𝑥) is NOT differentiable is {~\{−1, 1, 2\} ~\{−2, −1, 1\} ~\{0, 1\} =\{−1, 0, 1\}}
