::GATE 2026 Set 1 Q52::[plain]Consider the following context-free grammar 𝐺. 𝑆 → 𝑎𝑏𝑎𝐴𝐵𝐴𝑏𝑏𝑎 𝐴 → 𝑎𝑎𝐵𝐵𝐴𝑏 | 𝑏𝐵𝑎𝑏𝑎𝑎 𝐵 → 𝑎𝐵𝑏 | 𝑎𝑏 In the above grammar, 𝑆 is the start symbol, 𝑎 and 𝑏 are terminal symbols, and 𝐴 and 𝐵 are non-terminal symbols. Let 𝐿(𝐺) be the language generated by the grammar 𝐺. For a string 𝑠 ∈ 𝐿(𝐺), let 𝑛1 (𝑠) be the number of 𝑎’s in 𝑠 and 𝑛2 (𝑠) be the number of 𝑏’s in 𝑠. Which of the following statements is/are true? {~%-100%There is a string 𝑠 ∈ 𝐿(𝐺) such that 𝑛1 (𝑠) < 𝑛2 (𝑠) ~%50.0%For every string 𝑠 ∈ 𝐿(𝐺), 𝑛1 (𝑠) ≥ 𝑛2 (𝑠) ~%-100%There is a string 𝑠 ∈ 𝐿(𝐺) such that 𝑛1 (𝑠) > 2𝑛2 (𝑠) ~%50.0%For every string 𝑠 ∈ 𝐿(𝐺), 𝑛1 (𝑠) ≤ 2𝑛2 (𝑠)}
####Source: GATE 2026 Set 1, Q52

