::GATE 2026 Set 1 Q53::[plain]Consider the following two syntax-directed definitions SDD1 and SDD2 for type declarations. SDD1 Grammar (G1) 𝐷 →𝑇𝑉 𝑇 → 𝑖𝑛𝑡 𝑇 → 𝑓𝑙𝑜𝑎𝑡 𝑉 → 𝑉1 𝑖𝑑 𝑉 → 𝑖𝑑 SDD2 Semantic Rules Grammar (G2) Semantic Rules 𝐷. 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒 \= 𝑇. 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒 𝑉. 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒 \= 𝑇. 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒 𝑇. 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒 \= 𝑖𝑛𝑡 𝑇. 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒 \= 𝑓𝑙𝑜𝑎𝑡 𝑉1 . 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒 \= 𝑉. 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒 𝑝𝑢𝑡(𝑖𝑑. 𝑒𝑛𝑡𝑟𝑦, 𝑉. 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒) 𝑝𝑢𝑡(𝑖𝑑. 𝑒𝑛𝑡𝑟𝑦, 𝑉. 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒) 𝐷 → 𝐷1 𝑖𝑑 𝐷. 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒 \= 𝐷1 . 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒 𝑝𝑢𝑡(𝑖𝑑. 𝑒𝑛𝑡𝑟𝑦, 𝐷1 . 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒) 𝐷 → 𝑇 𝑖𝑑 𝐷. 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒 \= 𝑇. 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒 𝑝𝑢𝑡(𝑖𝑑. 𝑒𝑛𝑡𝑟𝑦, 𝑇. 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒) 𝑇 → 𝑖𝑛𝑡 𝑇 → 𝑓𝑙𝑜𝑎𝑡 𝑇. 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒 \= 𝑖𝑛𝑡 𝑇. 𝑡𝑦𝑝𝑒 \= 𝑓𝑙𝑜𝑎𝑡 𝐷 is the start symbol, and 𝑖𝑛𝑡, 𝑓𝑙𝑜𝑎𝑡 and 𝑖𝑑 are the three terminals. The non-terminal 𝑉1 is the same as 𝑉 and the non-terminal 𝐷1 is the same as 𝐷. Here, the subscript is used to differentiate the grammar symbols on the two sides of a production. The function 𝑝𝑢𝑡 updates the symbol table with the type information for an identifier. Let P and Q be the languages specified by grammars G1 and G2, respectively. Which of the following statements is/are true? {~%33.33%The languages P and Q are the same ~%33.33%SDD2 is S-attributed and contains only synthesized attributes ~%-100%SDD1 is L-attributed and contains only inherited attributes ~%33.33%The specifications of SDD1 and SDD2 are such that the same entries get added to the symbol table}
####Source: GATE 2026 Set 1, Q53

